questions.explanation:
[Correct - title "Asia Sports Vehicle Summary";
title2 "Distribution by Make";]: TITLE sets the first title line and TITLE2 adds a second line beneath it without replacing the first, producing both required lines.

[Incorrect - title "Asia Sports Vehicle Summary";
title "Distribution by Make";]: A second TITLE statement (not TITLE2) replaces the first title rather than adding a second line beneath it, so this produces only 'Distribution by Make', not both lines.

[Incorrect - title "Asia Sports Vehicle Summary";
subtitle "Distribution by Make";]: SUBTITLE is not a valid SAS titling statement — additional title lines are added with TITLE2, TITLE3, and so on.

[Incorrect - title "Distribution by Make";]: A single TITLE statement replaces all previous titles, so this alone would drop the first title line 'Asia Sports Vehicle Summary' instead of adding a second line.

questions.question_text:
The following SAS program is submitted:
data WORK.NEW;
year=2011;
amount=5000;
do i=1 to 5;
year=year+1;
do qtr=1 to 4;
amount=amount*1.1;
end;
end;
run;
proc print data=WORK.NEW noobs;
run;
Which output is correct?

An analyst wants to keep only warehouse observations where a newly computed variable, days_in_stock, exceeds 30. Two candidate DATA steps are shown:
data logistics.aged; set logistics.stock; where days_in_stock > 30; days_in_stock = today() - received_date; run;
data logistics.aged; set logistics.stock; days_in_stock = today() - received_date; if days_in_stock > 30; run;
Which two statements are true about these two approaches?
(Choose two.)

Assume that Work.Ds1 and Work.Ds2 exist and the following SAS program is submitted:
ods pdf file='results.pdf';
proc print data=work.ds1;
run;
proc freq data=work.ds1;
proc freq data=work.ds2;
run;
ods pdf close;
How many PDF files are created?

A farm-management DATA step computes yield per acre:
data farm.yield_summary; set farm.yield_raw; yield_per_acre = total_yield / acres; run;
Some observations have a missing value for acres. What happens for those observations, and what appears in the log?

An HR analyst builds a mailing extract from the full HR master, wanting only four specific columns and a friendlier name for the zip code column.
data mailing_extract; set hr_master(keep=emp_id first_name last_name mail_zip rename=(mail_zip=zip)); run;
What do the KEEP= and RENAME= data set options together accomplish here?

A coffee shop's raw sales extract stores the column unit_price as character data (for example, the text value "3.50") because it was imported from a point-of-sale export. A programmer submits the following DATA step:
data work.receipts;
set work.posraw;
total_due = qty * unit_price;
run;
The SAS log shows: NOTE: Character values have been converted to numeric values at the places given by: (Line):(Column). No ERROR or WARNING appears anywhere else in the log.
What does this NOTE mean for the DATA step?

Two files list the same 200 employees in identical row order: one with base salary, one with a bonus percentage. A programmer wants a single file with both figures per employee.
data payroll_full; merge base_salary bonus_pct; run;
Since there is no BY statement, how does SAS combine observations from base_salary and bonus_pct here?

A telecom billing DATA step computes:
data telecom.contracts; set telecom.contract_raw; months_elapsed = intck('month', contract_start, contract_end); run;
What does intck('month', contract_start, contract_end) calculate?

The following SAS program is submitted:
proc means data=work.schools median;
